@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Strings in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">

    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Strings in Java</h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn how Java represents text using String, how strings are
                created and compared, and how to use common String methods
                efficiently.
            </p>
        </div>


        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What is a String?</h3>

            <p>
                A <code>String</code> represents a sequence of characters.
                In Java, String is a class provided by the
                <code>java.lang</code> package.
            </p>

            <p>
                Strings are objects, even though Java provides special syntax
                for creating them using string literals.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String name = "CIIT";

System.out.println(name);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>CIIT</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- STRING CREATION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Creating Strings</h3>

            <p>
                Strings are commonly created using string literals.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String language = "Java";

String message = "Welcome CIIT world 🌍..!";</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                A String can also be explicitly created using the constructor,
                although literals are normally preferred.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = new String("Java");</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- IMMUTABILITY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String Immutability</h3>

            <p>
                Strings in Java are immutable. Once a String object is
                created, its character contents cannot be changed.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "CIIT";

text = text + " Institute";

System.out.println(text);</code></pre>

            </div>
<p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>CIIT Institute</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The original String object is not modified. A new String
                object is created for the concatenated result, and the
                variable is updated to reference it.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- STRING POOL -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String Pool</h3>

            <p>
                Java maintains a special pool for string literals. Identical
                literals can share the same pooled String object.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String first = "CIIT";

String second = "Institute";

System.out.println(first == second);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>false</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The <code>==</code> comparison here checks whether the two
                variables refer to the same object.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- EQUALS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Comparing Strings with equals()
            </h3>

            <p>
                Use <code>equals()</code> when you want to compare String
                contents.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String first = "CIIT";

String second = new String("Institute");

System.out.println(first.equals(second));</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>true</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- EQUALS IGNORE CASE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Case-Insensitive Comparison
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String first = "ciit";

String second = "INSTITUTE";

System.out.println(
    first.equalsIgnoreCase(second)
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>true</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- LENGTH -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String length()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>length()</code> method returns the number of UTF-16
                code units in the String.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "CIIT";

System.out.println(text.length());</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>4</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- CHARAT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">charAt()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>charAt()</code> method returns the character at a
                specified zero-based index.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Java";

System.out.println(text.charAt(0));

System.out.println(text.charAt(2));</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>J
v</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SUBSTRING -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">substring()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>substring()</code> method extracts part of a String.
                The ending index is exclusive.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Programming";

System.out.println(
    text.substring(0, 4)
);

System.out.println(
    text.substring(4)
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Prog
ramming</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- CONTAINS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">contains()</h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Java Programming";

System.out.println(
    text.contains("Java")
);

System.out.println(
    text.contains("Python")
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
false</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STARTS ENDS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                startsWith() and endsWith()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String file = "report.pdf";

System.out.println(
    file.startsWith("report")
);

System.out.println(
    file.endsWith(".pdf")
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
true</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- INDEX -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                indexOf() and lastIndexOf()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Java Programming";

System.out.println(
    text.indexOf("a")
);

System.out.println(
    text.lastIndexOf("a")
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>1
14</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- REPLACE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">replace()</h3>

            <p>
                Since Strings are immutable, <code>replace()</code> returns a
                new String instead of modifying the original.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Java is easy";

String updated =
    text.replace("easy", "powerful");

System.out.println(updated);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java is powerful</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TO LOWER -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                toLowerCase() and toUpperCase()
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Java Programming";

System.out.println(
    text.toLowerCase()
);

System.out.println(
    text.toUpperCase()
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>java programming
JAVA PROGRAMMING</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TRIM -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                trim() and strip()
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>trim()</code> removes certain leading and trailing
                characters with code points at or below U+0020.
                Modern Java also provides <code>strip()</code>, which uses
                Unicode-aware whitespace rules.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "   Java   ";

System.out.println(
    text.trim()
);

System.out.println(
    text.strip()
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java
Java</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>


        <!-- CONCATENATION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                String Concatenation
            </h3>

            <p>
                Strings can be combined using the <code>+</code> operator.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String firstName = "Samadhan";

String lastName = "Patole";

String fullName =
    firstName + " " + lastName;

System.out.println(fullName);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Samadhan Patole</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- CONCAT METHOD -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">concat()</h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String first = "CIIT";

String second = " Institute";

String result = first.concat(second);

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>CIIT Institute</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- SPLIT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">split()</h3>

            <p>
                The <code>split()</code> method divides a String according to
                a regular expression.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String languages =
    "Java,Python,CSharp";

String[] values =
    languages.split(",");

for (String value : values) {

    System.out.println(value);

}</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java
Python
CSharp</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- JOIN -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String.join()</h3>

            <p>
                <code>String.join()</code> can combine multiple strings using
                a delimiter.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String result = String.join(
    ", ",
    "Java",
    "Python",
    "CSharp"
);

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java, Python, CSharp</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- FORMAT -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String Formatting</h3>

            <p>
                <code>String.format()</code> creates formatted text using
                placeholders.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String name = "Amit";

int age = 25;

String result = String.format(
    "Name: %s, Age: %d",
    name,
    age
);

System.out.println(result);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Name: Amit, Age: 25</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TEXT BLOCK -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Text Blocks
            </h3>

            <p>
                Modern Java supports text blocks for convenient representation
                of multiline text.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String message = """
        Hello
        Welcome to Java
        Programming
        """;</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STRINGBUILDER -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                StringBuilder
            </h3>

            <p>
                When text needs to be modified repeatedly, use
                <code>StringBuilder</code> instead of creating many temporary
                String objects.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>StringBuilder builder =
    new StringBuilder();

builder.append("Java");
builder.append(" ");
builder.append("Programming");

System.out.println(
    builder.toString()
);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java Programming</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STRINGBUFFER -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                StringBuffer
            </h3>

            <p>
                <code>StringBuffer</code> is another mutable character
                sequence. Its methods are synchronized, making it different
                from <code>StringBuilder</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>StringBuffer buffer =
    new StringBuffer("Java");

buffer.append(" Programming");

System.out.println(buffer);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java Programming</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- STRING VS STRINGBUILDER -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer
            </h3>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>String</th>
                            <th>StringBuilder</th>
                            <th>StringBuffer</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Mutable</td>
                            <td>No</td>
                            <td>Yes</td>
                            <td>Yes</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Typical Use</td>
                            <td>Fixed text</td>
                            <td>Repeated text modification</td>
                            <td>Mutable text with synchronized methods</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Synchronization</td>
                            <td>Immutable</td>
                            <td>No</td>
                            <td>Yes</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Performance</td>
                            <td>Good for immutable values</td>
                            <td>Usually preferred for local mutable building</td>
                            <td>More overhead than StringBuilder</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- EMPTY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Checking Empty and Blank Strings
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "";

System.out.println(text.isEmpty());

String value = "   ";

System.out.println(value.isBlank());</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>true
true</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                <code>isEmpty()</code> checks whether the String has zero
                characters. <code>isBlank()</code> also considers strings
                containing only whitespace to be blank.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- NULL -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String and null</h3>

            <p>
                A String variable can contain <code>null</code>, meaning it
                does not currently reference a String object.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = null;

if (text != null) {

    System.out.println(text.length());

}</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

                <strong>Important:</strong>

                Calling an instance method such as <code>length()</code> on a
                null reference causes <code>NullPointerException</code>.

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- CHARACTERS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                String and Unicode
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java Strings use UTF-16 internally. Characters outside the
                Basic Multilingual Plane may require a surrogate pair, so
                <code>char</code> is not always equivalent to one Unicode
                code point.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = "Hello";

System.out.println(
    text.codePointAt(0)
);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>72</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- REAL WORLD -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Username Validation
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>String username = "  Sam2210  ";

username = username.strip();

if (!username.isBlank()
        &amp;&amp; username.length() &gt;= 5) {

    System.out.println(
        "Valid username"
    );

} else {

    System.out.println(
        "Invalid username"
    );

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>
    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Valid username</code></pre>
</div>

        </section>


        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">String Best Practices</h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>equals()</code> for content comparison.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid using <code>==</code> when your intention is to
                    compare String contents.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Remember that Strings are immutable.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>StringBuilder</code> for repeated local string
                    modifications.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Handle possible <code>null</code> references safely.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use <code>strip()</code> when Unicode-aware whitespace
                    handling is desired.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERVIEW -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Interview Questions
            </h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="stringQuestions">

                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#stringQuestionOne">

                            Why is String immutable in Java?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="stringQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#stringQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            String immutability supports safe sharing,
                            predictable behavior, and enables optimizations
                            such as string pooling.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#stringQuestionTwo">

                            What is the difference between == and equals()?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="stringQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#stringQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            For object references, <code>==</code> compares
                            references, while <code>equals()</code> is used
                            to compare logical content when the class
                            implements it appropriately.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#stringQuestionThree">

                            String vs StringBuilder?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="stringQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#stringQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            String is immutable. StringBuilder is mutable and
                            is generally useful when repeatedly constructing or
                            modifying text within a single thread.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#stringQuestionFour">

                            What is the String pool?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="stringQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#stringQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            It is the JVM's mechanism for sharing String
                            literals so identical literals can reuse the same
                            String object.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#stringQuestionFive">

                            What is the difference between isEmpty() and isBlank()?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="stringQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#stringQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            <code>isEmpty()</code> checks for zero length,
                            while <code>isBlank()</code> also considers a
                            string containing only whitespace to be blank.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


